1
00:00:08,350 --> 00:00:16,130
 I'm going to tell you a story now of a man who was almost always alone, and he was courageous, tenacious, even an obstinate man.

2
00:00:16,130 --> 00:00:22,490
 In the face of his opponents, he was an example to us all, and his works equal some of the greatest explorers, but his name is less famous.

3
00:00:23,070 --> 00:00:28,110
 Robert Cavalier de La Salle was 25 when he saw Canada for the first time, and he was fascinated.

4
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:31,050
 Here, adventure was waiting around every corner.

5
00:00:31,470 --> 00:00:34,990
 To answer this call to adventure, he abandoned the robes of the Jesuits.

6
00:00:35,390 --> 00:00:38,910
 Yes, he had become a Jesuit to please his father, but now his father was dead.

7
00:00:39,590 --> 00:00:42,550
 But on many occasions, he was to test the old saying,

8
00:00:42,690 --> 00:00:45,710
 who dares to abandon the company of Jesus

9
00:00:45,710 --> 00:00:50,390
 will be hounded with disaster throughout his whole life.

10
00:01:11,570 --> 00:01:26,590
 It was to become Montreal.

11
00:01:27,410 --> 00:01:30,210
 The Sulpician brothers have agricultural lands there

12
00:01:30,210 --> 00:01:31,870
 and large concessions will be granted.

13
00:01:32,150 --> 00:01:33,390
 A place called La Chine.

14
00:01:34,150 --> 00:01:35,850
 And they work very hard.

15
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:46,020
 Conditions have changed a lot since Champlain's time.

16
00:01:46,020 --> 00:01:49,760
 Les Cabot's instruction was now bearing fruit.

17
00:01:50,510 --> 00:01:55,090
 Now they didn't have to wait anxiously for a boat from France to bring them food for their survival.

18
00:01:55,790 --> 00:02:01,180
 There were only a few thousand French, and here they were outnumbered three to one by the Dutch,

19
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:03,200
 and twenty to one by the English.

20
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:10,910
 In this colony's commercial interest, Peter Stuyvesant, the governor of Manhattan,

21
00:02:11,390 --> 00:02:14,610
 agreed to give the Mohawk Indians 400 rifles.

22
00:02:15,070 --> 00:02:17,600
 Allies of other tribes in the Iroquois nation,

23
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:20,940
 they wasted no time in eliminating their rivals, the Hurons.

24
00:02:21,940 --> 00:02:25,920
 So the Hurons begged the French to help them,

25
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:30,900
 To take them into their protection, as they had been promised, and at least to give them weapons.

26
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:32,400
 But it was all in vain.

27
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:37,960
 Before, the forces were just about equal between the tribes.

28
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:41,980
 But now, well, those 400 Dutch rifles tipped the balance.

29
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:46,000
 Coupled with the French refusal to help, the Hurons were crushed.

30
00:02:46,610 --> 00:02:48,730
 This destabilized the whole region.

31
00:02:48,730 --> 00:02:52,690
 The whole equilibrium of North America was like a house of cards.

32
00:02:53,620 --> 00:02:56,160
 The Dutch helped themselves to Swedish territory.

33
00:03:00,590 --> 00:03:10,710
 the English in turn took over the Dutch territories. The French kept their heads

34
00:03:10,710 --> 00:03:14,610
 down and stayed in their fortresses. About a hundred were killed or

35
00:03:14,610 --> 00:03:20,730
 disappeared. Meanwhile, the vicar of Rouen, a Sulpicien, and the vicar of Rome, a

36
00:03:20,730 --> 00:03:26,010
 Jesuit, were fighting for supremacy. The Jesuit, Monseigneur de Laval, who was to

37
00:03:26,010 --> 00:03:30,010
 emerge the winner, consecrated a bishop. He swiftly replaced the excellent

38
00:03:30,010 --> 00:03:33,510
 governor, who didn't happen to be a friend. The new one, Monseigneur de

39
00:03:33,510 --> 00:03:39,090
 Courcel and the Intendant Talon were excellent fellows and with them at last a real regiment of

40
00:03:39,090 --> 00:03:45,040
 a thousand men was formed. Now things would be different. These daughters of the king we're

41
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:50,020
 waiting for are from excellent families, good breeding, often orphans. They'll make excellent

42
00:03:50,020 --> 00:03:54,520
 wives for you with royal dowries, of course. If you marry one of them, you will be given lands to

43
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:58,740
 cultivate. You will prosper and the right to trade in skins, hunting and fishing rights.

44
00:03:59,550 --> 00:04:24,130
 We can use this ship and supply grain and vast quantities of fish to our territories off in the Antilles, where they are needed.

45
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:27,990
 I know what I'm going to build and ride on the lakes, I hope.

46
00:04:28,110 --> 00:04:29,250
 Really? Ride on the lakes?

47
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:34,490
 Yes, at first. Yes, but later on, if I can believe what the Indians say, it'll...

48
00:04:34,490 --> 00:04:35,470
 Huh? What?

49
00:04:35,970 --> 00:04:38,230
 Ah, splendid, my good friend, if that's what you want.

50
00:04:38,680 --> 00:04:41,790
 You have my permission to explore the West. I grant you a commission.

51
00:04:45,850 --> 00:04:48,150
 Mr. Colbert, sir.

52
00:04:49,820 --> 00:04:56,860
 Jesuits whose piety and zeal have done much for the people of this land have gone beyond the limits set down for them.

53
00:04:57,320 --> 00:05:01,980
 They will resort to any means to eliminate, to discredit anyone who is not of their opinion or one of their own.

54
00:05:02,280 --> 00:05:05,980
 You must therefore guarantee that no undue number exceeds the high posts in future,

55
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:10,080
 but take care not to arouse Jesuit suspicion or appear that you are blaming them in any way never.

56
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:11,560
 Signed, Colbert, King's Minister.

57
00:05:14,720 --> 00:05:19,880
 Monsieur, with your permission, I would present the brothers Marquette and Joliet.

58
00:05:20,260 --> 00:05:22,770
 They are experienced explorers.

59
00:05:23,170 --> 00:05:26,230
 They have been granted a mission to explore territories to the west

60
00:05:26,230 --> 00:05:28,990
 for the propagation of the faith and the glory of the king.

61
00:05:29,730 --> 00:05:35,560
 Well, it's July 1669, and here's the first expedition headed by the Cavalier de la Salle.

62
00:05:35,820 --> 00:05:40,920
 Two Sulpicians, the Abbe Galinet and Dolier are there, and some Indian friends.

63
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:45,300
 And here they're sailing up the St. Lawrence and crossing Lake Ontario.

64
00:06:07,680 --> 00:06:33,420
 Mr. de la Salle, oh yes, a few days before you did, I went all the way to Lake Superior.

65
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:36,200
 We have heard there is a copper mine there.

66
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:38,300
 The governor ordered us to find it.

67
00:06:43,500 --> 00:06:46,560
 Listen, the Abbé Jolier has decided to go on all the way to Lake Erie.

68
00:06:46,720 --> 00:06:48,320
 We would quite like to accompany him.

69
00:06:48,550 --> 00:06:49,970
 We wondered if you would join us.

70
00:06:50,080 --> 00:06:52,580
 You could see I'm not well. I'm not able to follow you.

71
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:54,120
 You had better go on without me.

72
00:06:54,940 --> 00:06:55,300
 Oh.

73
00:06:58,060 --> 00:07:04,430
 Both of them? Let's go.

74
00:07:29,470 --> 00:07:44,640
 Alone, exhausted, and with no food in a hostile land 2,000 kilometers from home.

75
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:50,080
 He was to live on herbs and hunting and on handouts from Indians that he would meet occasionally.

76
00:07:50,080 --> 00:07:52,660
 and this continued for two years before he would return.

77
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,540
 Do you know many men who would have endured that?

78
00:07:56,350 --> 00:07:59,380
 But he was a strong, hardy fellow de La Salle,

79
00:07:59,480 --> 00:08:03,800
 and early in 1672, two and a half years after his departure,

80
00:08:03,940 --> 00:08:05,220
 he returned to the lobby.

81
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:15,820
 Frontenac, the new governor, arrived in Quebec.

82
00:08:16,240 --> 00:08:18,780
 He's a man of high qualities and a distinguished soldier,

83
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:19,880
 loyal and brave.

84
00:08:20,180 --> 00:08:22,420
 It's certain that he and de La Salle would be pleased,

85
00:08:22,540 --> 00:08:23,120
 but not his enemies.

86
00:08:23,340 --> 00:08:25,740
 On your expeditions, you must go in force, excellency.

87
00:08:25,860 --> 00:08:26,980
 I know Indians well, sir.

88
00:08:27,020 --> 00:08:27,960
 You must impress them.

89
00:08:27,960 --> 00:08:33,980
 they are highly impressionable believe me right here is where i think we must construct our fort

90
00:08:33,980 --> 00:08:38,800
 i'll call it frontenac it will be the first permanent link in a chain which should mean

91
00:08:38,800 --> 00:08:43,410
 we can maintain french domination here sir my lad be on your way as we're going to build fort

92
00:08:43,410 --> 00:08:47,970
 frontenac and you work well go and prepare it for my visit very important that now and remember

93
00:08:47,970 --> 00:08:54,830
 to build a good road between ville marie and lachine it's really the best time for such a

94
00:08:54,830 --> 00:09:00,690
 journey you are aware your excellency a number of dutch vessels sailed from manhattan to attack

95
00:09:00,690 --> 00:09:05,050
 Quebec, your place is here along with your men, not going off on adventures.

96
00:09:05,050 --> 00:09:09,070
 Thank you for your opinion, Father de Blon. I will give the matter my consideration.

97
00:09:09,070 --> 00:09:53,370
 Sal has worked well. 200 Iroquois chiefs are assembled to meet us.

98
00:09:53,370 --> 00:10:27,900
 Two peoples can bring mutual friendship and prosperity together for centuries.

99
00:10:27,900 --> 00:10:46,690
 The 14th of France was hesitant about expanding to the west.

100
00:10:46,690 --> 00:10:51,360
 So de Lassalle, wanting to keep his options open, went to Paris for a private meeting with Colbert,

101
00:10:51,360 --> 00:10:54,000
 but a prior visitor had been the Jesuit Father Raguenot.

102
00:10:54,000 --> 00:11:01,240
 This young man is dangerous. To satisfy his ambition, he wouldn't hesitate to set one against the other of the tribes, yes, even to creating wars and massacres.

103
00:11:01,360 --> 00:11:03,550
 Then we shall have to find a way to pacify them.

104
00:11:03,690 --> 00:11:06,160
 This is most serious, what you've told me, and something has to be done.

105
00:11:06,180 --> 00:11:08,980
 He is quite mad. The man's a menace to anybody he meets.

106
00:11:09,300 --> 00:11:11,730
 It was a wicked thing to say. What made him do that?

107
00:11:11,810 --> 00:11:15,610
 The Jesuits wanted credit for most of the big discoveries being made, and that's why.

108
00:11:15,710 --> 00:11:17,950
 Some say they intended to join the Spanish down in the south.

109
00:11:18,230 --> 00:11:19,630
 And de La Salle, what happened to him?

110
00:11:19,630 --> 00:11:21,630
 Months went by, but he refused to see him.

111
00:11:21,730 --> 00:11:25,050
 However, one day, the Prince de Conte intervened on his behalf.

112
00:11:25,980 --> 00:11:30,110
 Don't you worry, believe me, he was to win right on down the line.

113
00:11:30,390 --> 00:11:32,530
 He would be commissioned to explore to the West,

114
00:11:32,830 --> 00:11:35,890
 and here authorized to build forts anywhere he thought it necessary,

115
00:11:36,070 --> 00:11:37,530
 and given all the men that he needed.

116
00:11:58,890 --> 00:12:20,540
 This is our route.

117
00:12:20,920 --> 00:12:22,900
 Eventually, we go into the Illinois River,

118
00:12:24,400 --> 00:12:27,300
 and then we sail to the Mississippi.

119
00:12:27,750 --> 00:12:30,350
 It's the truth, my lad, this ship's hauling some distance.

120
00:12:30,740 --> 00:12:32,490
 That's true, 3,000 kilometers.

121
00:12:32,490 --> 00:12:36,440
 And we'll construct our fortifications there, there, there,

122
00:12:36,540 --> 00:12:37,280
 And there.

123
00:12:42,230 --> 00:12:50,770
 Hey, what are you doing here?

124
00:12:51,350 --> 00:12:54,220
 I'm just building boats now.

125
00:12:54,640 --> 00:12:56,070
 Haven't I dealt fairly with you?

126
00:12:56,330 --> 00:12:58,670
 Didn't I give you a substantial advance on your salaries?

127
00:12:58,940 --> 00:13:00,460
 I want an explanation!

128
00:13:00,780 --> 00:13:02,700
 Of course, Matthew, he's going to explain it to you.

129
00:13:02,840 --> 00:13:06,180
 When we got to Krebker, they told us it was crazy trying to build boats here

130
00:13:06,180 --> 00:13:08,420
 and the Griffin would never make it this far

131
00:13:08,420 --> 00:13:11,220
 and we shouldn't try to stay here because those Indians were hostile

132
00:13:11,220 --> 00:13:12,860
 and we'd all be killed.

133
00:13:13,550 --> 00:13:16,790
 So we figured that the best thing that we could do was get away.

134
00:13:16,790 --> 00:13:18,650
 But all the other, the six others deserted,

135
00:13:18,810 --> 00:13:21,070
 took away a thousand pounds of supplies with them.

136
00:13:22,210 --> 00:13:23,510
 Now you tell me, who said that?

137
00:13:24,510 --> 00:13:26,930
 I can't really tell you who said it.

138
00:13:27,410 --> 00:13:29,210
 And in which direction did the deserters go?

139
00:13:30,060 --> 00:13:32,000
 He asked you which direction they took.

140
00:13:32,120 --> 00:13:35,170
 Come on, you answer, or I'll whittle your ears to points.

141
00:13:35,310 --> 00:13:36,450
 No, I thought you said, Marie.

142
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:39,410
 It's my pleasure to go and capture all of them.

143
00:13:39,630 --> 00:13:42,040
 If you haven't come back and we're ready to sail,

144
00:13:42,220 --> 00:13:43,520
 you come and join us at Miamis.

145
00:13:44,830 --> 00:13:50,110
 Those are our trappers, gone over a year.

146
00:13:50,520 --> 00:13:52,230
 I hope that they bring in plenty of skins.

147
00:13:52,230 --> 00:13:59,240
 Next stop at Michilimackinac, understand?

148
00:13:59,640 --> 00:14:01,900
 You'll deliver everything that we can't take.

149
00:14:02,400 --> 00:14:06,000
 Once you've done that, go on to Fort Conti and unload the cargo.

150
00:14:06,360 --> 00:14:08,010
 Then you return as quickly as you can.

151
00:14:08,110 --> 00:14:11,070
 Yeah, you can trust me, sir. I'll carry out your orders to the letter.

152
00:14:15,160 --> 00:14:21,420
 Somehow I'm troubled by that pilot. I'm sure I've seen him before.

153
00:14:21,520 --> 00:14:23,900
 He reminds me of someone, but I can't place him. I mean, it's trouble.

154
00:14:23,980 --> 00:14:27,000
 The man is a good pilot, and I must do everything I can to protect the supplies.

155
00:14:27,100 --> 00:14:28,560
 We'll be needing them if we're going to succeed.

156
00:14:28,700 --> 00:14:32,460
 It's only a premonition, no more. Besides, it's your decision. You're the man who's in charge.

157
00:14:40,960 --> 00:14:50,140
 And it's to capture them.

158
00:14:50,140 --> 00:14:57,770
 I did run surters with some of their booty only. No sign of the boat. Just vanish off the lake.

159
00:14:58,270 --> 00:16:04,900
 Nah, I won't say I told you so, but nobody can go down to Mighty Chief's words.

160
00:16:05,240 --> 00:16:08,800
 Those who told you to counsel me, they all speak with forked tongue.

161
00:16:10,310 --> 00:16:13,220
 This night, I was not sleeping when Miami Monso came.

162
00:16:13,380 --> 00:16:15,120
 Yes, he along with all the others.

163
00:16:15,340 --> 00:16:18,660
 I know they brought you four cooking pots, eight hatchets, and twenty knives,

164
00:16:18,780 --> 00:16:20,020
 all of which you quickly buried.

165
00:16:20,320 --> 00:16:21,820
 They are presents of false friends.

166
00:16:22,240 --> 00:16:25,600
 Noble spirits speak in the light of day, as we do, not in the dark of night, hiding.

167
00:16:25,600 --> 00:16:27,440
 Are we enemies of our brothers, the Illinois?

168
00:16:27,780 --> 00:16:28,200
 Are we?

169
00:16:28,610 --> 00:16:30,850
 Do we come with weapons to make war on you?

170
00:16:36,940 --> 00:16:43,280
 No more cord or nails. Can't do anything without them.

171
00:16:44,070 --> 00:16:46,460
 I'll go to Fort Frontenac and see what's going on.

172
00:16:46,750 --> 00:16:48,930
 I'll return with reinforcements and supplies.

173
00:16:49,900 --> 00:16:52,200
 Going in winter, you'd have to be crazy to try it.

174
00:16:52,370 --> 00:16:54,280
 Don't worry, I'm used to doing it.

175
00:16:54,750 --> 00:16:57,660
 You will guard the fort at Crevcourt until I come back.

176
00:16:58,380 --> 00:17:01,360
 And then our friends set off on an imbecilic journey once again,

177
00:17:01,480 --> 00:17:04,140
 2,000 kilometers, but this time with a handful of men.

178
00:17:04,560 --> 00:17:08,340
 If I told you every adventure they had, we'd be here till past midnight.

179
00:17:08,340 --> 00:17:10,340
 And maybe even till dawn.

180
00:17:10,850 --> 00:17:22,240
 Anyway, finally arriving, you can imagine in what state, arriving at Fort Frontenac,

181
00:17:22,660 --> 00:17:24,660
 Tonti's news reached his ears.

182
00:17:24,960 --> 00:17:27,560
 Meanwhile, when the cat's away, the mice will play,

183
00:17:27,680 --> 00:17:31,860
 and a revolt breaks out at Creve Coeur, and there seems to be somebody behind it.

184
00:17:32,710 --> 00:17:33,190
 Oh.

185
00:17:34,660 --> 00:17:39,350
 Some deserters have been spotted at Lake Ontario.

186
00:17:40,280 --> 00:17:42,480
 It looks like they went that way. After them!

187
00:17:58,190 --> 00:18:10,440
 What is my witness? Is this a sign to the bottom?

188
00:18:10,900 --> 00:18:12,470
 Yeah, and the cargo. Where is that?

189
00:18:12,510 --> 00:18:14,010
 It was sheared out. That was the bargain.

190
00:18:14,050 --> 00:18:14,240
 What?

191
00:18:14,240 --> 00:18:18,530
 In a word, returning to Creve Coeur on January 25, 1681,

192
00:18:18,590 --> 00:18:23,590
 One, the Cavalier de la Salle, together with his small band, embarked on the great voyage of their lives.

193
00:18:24,070 --> 00:18:29,050
 Finding the Mississippi, there were 22 Frenchmen, 18 Indian men, 10 Indian women, and even three children.

194
00:18:29,950 --> 00:18:32,330
 Hey, wait! Wait! Wait for me!

195
00:18:34,440 --> 00:18:55,310
 Wait! Wait! Join up with it!

196
00:19:10,400 --> 00:19:30,590
 After after we reach the sea yet? No, just more the same.

197
00:19:34,230 --> 00:19:36,020
 Soldier, the ocean must be near.

198
00:19:44,890 --> 00:19:53,320
 His Majesty, Louis XIV, King of France and Navarre, hereby claim this land, Louisiana.

199
00:19:53,880 --> 00:20:01,020
 The brave explorer finally had a triumph.

200
00:20:01,930 --> 00:20:03,310
 It would be altogether too simple.

201
00:20:03,430 --> 00:20:05,950
 The trouble was, the new governor, a simpleton named Delabarre,

202
00:20:06,050 --> 00:20:08,350
 wrote to King Louis that all of Dassault's discoveries were useless.

203
00:20:08,490 --> 00:20:09,530
 He was kicked out of Canada.

204
00:20:10,050 --> 00:20:10,970
 Wasn't right.

205
00:20:11,910 --> 00:20:15,310
 Returning to Versailles, Robert did manage to convince the king

206
00:20:15,310 --> 00:20:17,170
 to name him governor of Louisiana.

207
00:20:17,650 --> 00:20:20,370
 Now, here he is with four ships and 300 volunteers

208
00:20:20,370 --> 00:20:23,630
 off to find the mouth of the Mississippi River and found a colony there.

209
00:20:23,810 --> 00:20:26,090
 He has under his command a certain ship's captain,

210
00:20:26,310 --> 00:20:29,480
 who was to be no help at all, Captain Beaujeu.

211
00:20:29,480 --> 00:20:32,820
 San Domingo will be a port of call. We'll unload the sick there.

212
00:20:33,930 --> 00:20:35,390
 Now then, set a course for Little Gold.

213
00:20:35,530 --> 00:20:37,320
 But that isn't what he said.

214
00:20:38,500 --> 00:20:40,260
 Do is he!

215
00:20:40,530 --> 00:20:41,250
 Do it!

216
00:20:44,700 --> 00:20:48,820
 Set a course for the west, otherwise the current will carry us too far east.

217
00:20:49,100 --> 00:20:52,900
 I would say the mouth ought to be right here. This is our course.

218
00:20:53,390 --> 00:20:56,270
 I still say you will drift too far east.

219
00:20:57,290 --> 00:21:00,510
 The best thing you can do, Captain, is button your lip.

220
00:21:00,790 --> 00:21:03,730
 You have already lost us one ship, and it was entirely your fault.

221
00:21:04,220 --> 00:21:08,160
 We're too far to the west, Captain. You will instantly order this ship to turn about.

222
00:21:08,160 --> 00:21:09,100
 You're here, Captain.

223
00:21:09,830 --> 00:21:14,050
 Captain, are you aware I have given you an order?

224
00:21:14,050 --> 00:21:26,180
 Just be a branch of the Delta, if you ask me.

225
00:21:26,180 --> 00:21:30,990
 Once again, de la Salle missed it, but one year after his departure and after endless searching,

226
00:21:30,990 --> 00:21:33,980
 he did discover the mouth of the Colorado River.

227
00:21:33,980 --> 00:21:36,860
 Over 500 kilometers west of the Mississippi.

228
00:21:36,860 --> 00:21:58,300
 Boy, de la Salle found his Mississippi and my missions.

229
00:21:58,300 --> 00:22:08,700
 There's a board, white anchor!

230
00:22:08,700 --> 00:22:16,790
 It's a hard voyage. We found the way up the Mississippi.

231
00:22:16,790 --> 00:22:21,930
 We'll go all the way up to Illinois, then come back down with reinforcements and plenty of supply.

232
00:22:21,930 --> 00:22:24,610
 Well, grant them your everlasting help, Lord.

233
00:22:24,610 --> 00:22:43,660
 Rule 1687, Robert de La Salle is assassinated together with two of his men, by one of his own bandits, said.

234
00:22:44,200 --> 00:22:46,010
 That was an awful way to end up.

235
00:22:46,440 --> 00:22:50,660
 You realize, one-third of the USA territory today was controlled by France?

236
00:22:50,870 --> 00:22:51,790
 What about the English?

237
00:22:52,090 --> 00:22:55,570
 Naturally, they didn't like it, and they outnumbered the French 20 to 1.

238
00:22:55,830 --> 00:23:00,290
 Yes, there would be the Battle of Fort Duquesne, where General Braddock would see his men drop like flies.

239
00:23:00,610 --> 00:23:04,270
 In the English offensive in Arcadia, the inhabitants were deported or shot.

240
00:23:04,270 --> 00:23:08,910
 That was called the great disturbance. Officially, France and England are not at war.

241
00:23:09,850 --> 00:23:15,310
 But this time it is war, and the English send their 30,000 soldiers to meet the French's 300.

242
00:23:16,110 --> 00:23:18,330
 Louisbourg falls, and Quebec is under siege.

243
00:23:19,010 --> 00:23:22,810
 General Wolfe manages to infiltrate his men onto the Plateau of Abraham.

244
00:23:23,190 --> 00:23:29,020
 Thought inaccessible, Wolfe, then Montcalm, defending a field, fall, and Quebec falls, and then Montreal.

245
00:23:30,610 --> 00:23:37,940
 Gives up Canada and Ohio to England, and for good measure, gives up Louisiana to Spain.

246
00:23:37,940 --> 00:23:52,050
 Oh, yes. And for 80 years, by the sheer willpower of the Cavalier de La Salle, it was French, but with Lafayette and Degrasse and Rochambeau, France was to accord these American revolutionaries the decisive help that resulted in the United States of America.

